WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.000
So in the late 1980s, Broward County Sheriff's Office decided that they just didn't have

00:04.000 --> 00:06.720
enough crack cocaine to use and undercover stings.

00:06.720 --> 00:07.840
So what was their solution?

00:07.840 --> 00:11.680
They set up shop on the 7th floor of the Broward County Courthouse in Fort Lauderdale,

00:11.680 --> 00:15.640
where they cooked crack using $20,000 worth of seized cocaine.

00:15.640 --> 00:21.320
They made crack rocks, packaged them individually, and then sold them to people during sting operations.

00:21.320 --> 00:25.040
At the time, those who were involved defended the practice, claiming it was efficient for

00:25.040 --> 00:28.200
making a rest and save time on lab tests.

00:28.200 --> 00:32.880
However, however, called it outrageous and unethical, defense attorneys were especially vocal,

00:32.880 --> 00:37.080
pointing out the moral issues of cops creating and distributing crack.

00:37.080 --> 00:37.920
Come on now.

00:37.920 --> 00:42.520
The story even shocked federal officials with some admitting that they had never heard of such practice before.

00:42.520 --> 00:44.160
Let's fast forward to today.

00:44.160 --> 00:46.880
And the fallout from the scheme is finally being addressed.

00:46.880 --> 00:51.240
Broward County State Attorney Harold F. Pire announced that his office is working to vacate

00:51.240 --> 00:55.360
as many as 2,600 convictions tied to these particular operations.

00:55.360 --> 00:59.480
Many of these convictions involve people who were not selling drugs but buying small amounts,

00:59.480 --> 01:03.080
often within 1,000 feet of schools which carried harsher penalties.

01:03.080 --> 01:07.760
The Florida Supreme Court, though, ruled in 1993 that the actions violated the state's

01:07.760 --> 01:11.960
due process clause, declaring an unconstitutional to charge people in cases where the drugs were

01:11.960 --> 01:14.760
manufactured and sold by police officers.

01:14.760 --> 01:19.600
And layman's turns, it's wrong for police departments to make crack and sell it to people

01:19.600 --> 01:20.840
in order to lock them up.

